{#-
  /run/proximos/control/config.yaml — the `radio:` section, read by control-radio's
  dispatch::load_config() (crates/control-radio/src/config.rs, DEFAULT_CONFIG_PATH).

  NOTHING PRODUCED THIS FILE BEFORE WP-C. radio-setup.service, proximosctl
  control.radio and (WP-D) proximos-control.service all read it, and every one of them
  reports NotBootstrapped without it — it is the hardware prerequisite for the whole
  LoRaWAN chain, not a convenience.

  WHY THIS TEMPLATE IS ONE SUBSTITUTION AND NOT A YAML LITERAL. The `radio:` schema is a
  tree with three named-map arms; a device overlay may add a fourth OTAA profile or
  change one field of one profile, and a template made of `{{ radio.device_profiles.
  otaa.default.uplink_interval }}` lines could express neither. So the merge happens on
  the parsed document (defaults.yaml ← overlay, mappings recursively, scalars and
  sequences replaced) and the result is re-serialized here as one block. The knobs, and
  the comments explaining every one of them, live in defaults.yaml — which is the file a
  human edits and which ships beside this one on the rootfs.

  Emitted in EVERY mode, `off` included: control-radio has to be able to read the mode in
  order to refuse a command with OffMode (exit 35). The chirpstack and forwarder configs
  are the ones the mode gates.

  `radio:` and `mqtt:`. monolith.md §9.1 has this file eventually carrying a `portal:`
  section too, once the merged proximos-control binary reads one config for the whole
  process (§11.4-§11.6); until then the portal keeps its own file.

  WHY `mqtt:` IS HERE (plan/portal/mqtt/design.md §9.2). The two halves must agree on one
  topic root, and two defaults in two files are not agreement — they are two things that
  can be edited separately. A half-flip is SILENT data loss: the portal's
  `extract_hostname` drops every topic whose root does not match, and its subscribe
  succeeds against a prefix nothing publishes, so nothing anywhere reports an error and
  the readings simply stop arriving. So the value the portal is rendered with is the value
  the agent is rendered with, emitted here from the one `portal.topic_prefix` key, and
  `control-core` reads this file in preference to its own `fiber.config.yaml`.

  `mqtt.broker` + `mqtt.tls` ONLY ON A CLIENT (plan/control/cluster.md §4.4). The agent's
  primary broker becomes the Server's TLS listener under this Hub's peer account, read the
  same way as `topic_prefix`, so the seeded fiber.config.yaml is never edited. Both blocks
  or neither: control-core applies the pair only when the TLS half is present and enabled,
  and refuses a non-loopback broker whose CA is missing rather than fall back to plaintext.

  NEVER THE PASSWORD ITSELF. This file is 0640 root:proximos, so proximos-control could
  read it. `password_file` names /data/proximos/cluster/peer_password (0600 root), which
  the agent reads because it runs as root; control-core refuses an override that carries an
  inline `password`.
-#}
# Rendered by `proximosctl render` (crates/proximos-render). Regenerated on
# every boot; edits made directly to this file are lost on the next render.
# Per-device overrides go in /data/proximos/config/proximos.config.yaml,
# merged over /usr/share/proximos/templates/defaults.yaml — which is also
# where every key below is documented.
{{ radio_yaml }}
mqtt:
  # One root for both halves — see the note at the top of this template.
  topic_prefix: "{{ topic_prefix }}"
{% if broker is not none %}
  # Client role: the Server's broker, from /data/proximos/cluster/.
  broker:
    host: {{ broker.host | tojson }}
    port: {{ broker.port }}
    username: {{ broker.username | tojson }}
    password_file: {{ broker.password_file | tojson }}
  tls:
    enabled: true
    ca_cert_path: {{ broker.ca_cert_path | tojson }}
{% endif %}
